    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, let's dive straight into the technical aspects of your walnut joinery issue. If you've already checked the bevel angles and they're correct, we can rule out that potential cause.\n\nMoisture content in the walnut could indeed be a factor. Wood that's too dry can be more prone to splintering, especially when you're working with fine details like dovetails. If the wood isn't properly acclimated to your workshop environment, it might be reacting to the changes in humidity. Try to ensure that your walnut is acclimated to your workshop's conditions before you start working on it.\n\nCutting against the grain is another common cause of splintering. When you're working with dovetails, it's crucial to maintain a consistent direction of the grain. If you're cutting against the grain, even slightly, it can cause the fibers to tear and splinter. Make sure you're always cutting in the same direction as the grain, and if you need to change direction, do so gradually to minimize the risk of splintering.\n\nIf you're still experiencing issues, it might be worth examining your technique more closely. Are you using the right amount of pressure when you're paring down the pins? Too much pressure can cause the wood to splinter. Also, consider the sharpness",
